<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Josquin des Prez</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1450–<a href="/years/1521">1521</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Josquin, Josquin Desprez, Josquin Lebloitte dit des Prez</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Josquin des Prez was a French or Franco-Flemish composer of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> and a central figure of the Franco-Flemish School, born around 1450 in present-day northeastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a>. A singer by training, he served <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rene-of-anjou">René of Anjou</a>, Cardinal Ascanio Sforza, Popes Innocent VIII and Alexander VI, Louis XII and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ercole-i-d-este">Ercole I d'Este</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferrara">Ferrara</a>, for whom he wrote the Missa Hercules Dux Ferrariae. His masses, motets and chansons developed an imitative polyphony of independent voices closely tied to the text, and many were printed by Ottaviano Petrucci. From 1504 he was provost of the collegiate church of Notre-Dame at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conde-sur-lescaut">Condé-sur-l'Escaut</a>, and he died in 1521.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/josquin-des-prez.jpg" alt="Josquin des Prez" width="330" height="336" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Josquin_woodcut.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Josquin</b> and Victoria used its tunes as the cantus firmus of Masses; Dufay, Palestrina, Byrd and others set it in the Renaissance; Monteverdi included it in his Vespers of 1610, and the Emperor Leopold I, Louis Couperin (1658) and Marc-Antoine Charpentier (four settings, 1670s–1680s) set it in the…</blockquote><a href="/songs/ave-maris-stella">Ave maris stella</a></li><li><blockquote>Its psalms have often been set in classical music, Psalm 114 by <b>Josquin des Prez</b> and Mendelssohn, verses of Psalm 115 by Bach in his cantata Der Herr denket an uns, Psalm 117 by Vivaldi and Mozart and Psalm 118 by Léonin and Pérotin.</blockquote><a href="/songs/hallel">Hallel</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Josquin</b>'s two Masses, the Missa L'homme armé super voces musicales and the Missa L'homme armé sexti toni, are among the best known, and Pipelare, Pierre de la Rue, Cristóbal de Morales, Du Fay and Palestrina each wrote more than one.</blockquote><a href="/songs/lhomme-arme">L'homme armé (The Armed Man)</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Josquin des Prez</b> built his Missa Pange lingua, one of his last works, dated to between 1515 and 1521 and published after his death, on the Roman tune; many commentators count it among the high points of Renaissance polyphony.</blockquote><a href="/songs/pange-lingua-gloriosi-corporis-mysterium">Pange lingua gloriosi corporis mysterium</a></li><li><blockquote>Victoria, Palestrina, <b>Josquin</b>, Lassus, Charpentier, Pergolesi, Alessandro Scarlatti, Vivaldi, Zelenka, Handel and Liszt all set the text; Schubert wrote no fewer than seven versions, Poulenc one in 1941, and Arvo Pärt one first performed in Essen Cathedral in 2002.</blockquote><a href="/songs/salve-regina">Salve Regina</a></li><li><blockquote>Composers from <b>Josquin des Prez</b> and Palestrina in the Renaissance, through Vivaldi, Pergolesi (1736), Haydn (1767) and Rossini (1831–1841), to Dvořák (1876–1877), Verdi (1897), Poulenc (1950) and Arvo Pärt (1985) have written settings, and a website devoted to them counts some 250.</blockquote><a href="/songs/stabat-mater">Stabat Mater</a></li></ul></section>
